Here is a C++ program that uses recursion to find the nth Fibonacci number:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

// Function to find the nth Fibonacci number using recursion
int find_Fibonacci(int n) {
    // Base case: If n is less than 2 return n
    if (n < 2) {
        return n;
    }
    // Recursive call: Find the previous Fibonacci number
    int previous_n = find_Fibonacci(n - 1);
    // Find the previous-to-previous Fibonacci number
    int previous_previous_n = find_Fibonacci(n - 2);
    // Return the sum of the previous two Fibonacci numbers
    return previous_n + previous_previous_n;
}

int main() {
    // Test the function
    int n;
    std::cout << "Enter the value of n:";
    std::cin >> n;
    std::cout << "The " << n << "th Fibonacci number is:";
    std::cout << find_Fibonacci(n);
    return 0;
}
```
This program takes n as an input from the user and uses the `find_Fibonacci` function to find the nth Fibonacci number using recursion. It then prints the result to the console. Note that this program assumes that the user enters a positive integer for n. If you want to handle negative integers or non-integer inputs, you can add additional error-checking code as needed.
